I can help you organize this so your meeting with Maria Santos is efficient and you’re prepared for the hearing. I’m not a lawyer, so treat the items below as practical preparation and question suggestions to review with your attorney.

First — a couple quick clarifying questions before I proceed further:
- Is the contract dispute and the immigration-court matter the same case or two separate matters? (They normally are different courts; I want to be sure.)
- Do you have the preliminary hearing date and any paperwork (complaint, Notice to Appear, summons, proof of service)? If so, what are the deadlines your lawyer already told you?

Suggested questions to ask Maria Santos (prioritized)
- Case/status & timeline
  - What is the exact procedural posture (what was filed, what claims/relief is sought, and what deadlines apply)?
  - What will happen at the preliminary hearing (purpose, likely questions, possible outcomes)?
  - What are the next deadlines and key dates if the case goes to trial?
- Strategy & likely legal issues
  - What is our overall strategy (settlement vs. trial)? What are the strengths and weaknesses?
  - Are there motions you recommend now (motion to dismiss, motions in limine, continuance, etc.)?
- Evidence & documentation
  - What documents do you need me to bring and in what format? (contracts, emails, texts, invoices, payment records, bank statements, performance records)
  - Which documents are most critical to establish my version of events?
  - Do any documents need authentication, notarization, or certification?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should we call as witnesses (names, roles) and who should we not call?
  - How should I prepare my witnesses (what questions to expect, what documents to bring)?
  - Should we get written witness statements or affidavits in advance?
- Responding/pleadings
  - Do I need to file a response or answer now? Can you confirm you will file it (and by when)?
  - Are there required disclosures or discovery I must complete? How long does discovery take?
- Cross-examination & credibility
  - What topics might opposing counsel focus on to undermine me?
  - What are the most damaging issues I should prepare to explain or mitigate?
- Immigration implications (if immigration court is involved)
  - Could the outcome of this dispute affect my immigration status? If so, how do we address that?
  - Should we coordinate with any immigration counsel or include immigration concerns in strategy?
- Settlement & negotiation
  - What settlement range is reasonable? What would you recommend as opening/acceptable offers?
  - If settlement is pursued, how does it affect immigration matters or other claims?
- Practical & logistics
  - What should I say (and avoid saying) in court or during negotiations?
  - Do you want me present at the preliminary hearing, and if so, what should I wear and bring?
  - What are estimated costs and fees if this proceeds to trial?
- Communication & next steps
  - How will you update me on developments and what do you need from me before the hearing?
  - Can you provide a checklist of documents and a timeline for submission?

Concrete questions the judge might ask (examples you can prepare short answers for)
- Identification and jurisdiction
  - “Please state your full name for the record.”
  - “Are you represented by counsel?” / “Is counsel present?” 
- Case basics & status
  - “What is the nature of your claim/defense?” (Brief factual summary)
  - “Has service been effected? Has a response been filed?”
- Factual timeline & key events
  - “When was the contract signed?” “What were the key terms?”
  - “When did the alleged breach occur? What did you do in response?”
- Procedural & scheduling
  - “Are you ready for scheduling/trial?” “Do you have any time conflicts?”
  - “Do you consent to a continuance/mediation?”
- Credibility / damages
  - “How were you harmed / what damages are you claiming?”
  - “Have you attempted to mitigate damages?”
- If immigration-related, judge may ask:
  - (Depends on the immigration judge) questions about status, appeals, or how the civil dispute ties into immigration issues. (Ask Maria for specifics.)

Common questions opposing counsel may ask on direct or cross-examination (examples)
- Contract terms & interpretation:
  - “Did you sign this contract?” “Did you read this clause?” “Who drafted it?”
- Performance & breach:
  - “What specific obligations did you fulfill?” “What did the other party fail to do?”
- Communications:
  - “Did you email/ text the other party on [date]? What did you say?” (Be prepared to authenticate and explain any written communications.)
- Motive & credibility:
  - “Why are you bringing this claim now?” “Have you made inconsistent statements previously?”
- Damages & mitigation:
  - “How did you calculate your damages?” “What steps did you take to limit losses?”
- Relationship with opposing party:
  - “What is/was your relationship with the opposing party?” (If ex-spouse is involved, expect careful questioning about motives, history, and any financial ties.)
- Prior conduct:
  - Questions about prior contracts, employment performance, or prior legal claims (ask Maria about how to handle questions about prior bad acts).

Documents and materials to bring to your meeting and to the hearing
- Pleadings served on you (summons, complaint, Notice to Appear if immigration)
- The contract(s) in question (original signed copies if available)
- All related communications: emails, texts, letters, messaging threads (export or print with dates)
- Payment records, invoices, payroll records, bank statements, cancelled checks
- Performance records, delivery receipts, work logs, photos, or other proof of performance or breach
- Any prior settlement offers, demand letters, or cease-and-desist correspondence
- List of witnesses with contact info and short statement of expected testimony
- Timeline (one-page chronological summary with dates and short descriptions)
- ID, counsel contact info, and any filings you’ve made or received
- If immigration matter: A-number, forms, prior immigration filings, evidence of status

How to prepare answers and behave at hearings (practical tips)
- Keep answers short, factual, and truthful. Don’t volunteer extra information.
- Practice a concise one- or two-sentence summary of your position and timeline.
- Review documents so you can quickly find dates and key clauses.
- Rehearse likely cross-examination questions with your attorney; practice staying calm and pausing before you answer.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so rather than guessing.
- Ask Maria how she will introduce evidence and whether she will lead you or call you as a witness.

Preparation tasks to ask Maria to handle and tasks for you
- Ask Maria to: confirm deadlines, prepare/serve filings, draft witness subpoenas, file motions, handle evidence admissibility issues, and prepare a question plan for cross-examination.
- Your tasks: gather and label documents, prepare a timeline, list and contact witnesses, write brief notes on each key event, and be ready to sign any authorizations if needed.

If you’d like, I can:
- Convert these suggestions into a one-page checklist you can bring to your meeting with Maria.
- Draft a short email to Maria summarizing what you want to discuss and asking for confirmation of deadlines and next steps (I can draft but will not send without your instruction).
- Create a short script and practice questions for a mock Q&A you can rehearse.

Which of those would you like next? And please answer the two clarifying questions above so I can tailor the list (especially regarding whether this is a civil contract case, an immigration matter, or both).

