Immediate steps (do these now)
- Contact Maria right away to confirm deadlines and the hearing date/time and request a prep meeting (mock Q&A if possible). You said you are represented, so don’t communicate with opposing counsel or the court directly without Maria’s approval.
- Preserve all relevant evidence (do not delete messages): emails, texts, contracts, invoices, bank records, performance reviews, pay stubs, timecards, personnel files, any termination letters, and anything exchanged with the opposing party or employer.
- Make a complete timeline of key dates (contract signed, performance dates, payment dates, termination or breach, when you were served, deadlines).
- Gather court papers you were served with (complaint, summons, any motions, notice of hearing). Bring originals and make copies.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
Procedural / immediate
- What deadlines apply now (time to respond to complaint, discovery deadlines, filing deadlines)?
- What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing? What issues will the judge decide at this hearing vs. at trial?
- Are there any emergency motions we should file (stay, protective order, etc.)?
- Do we need to request a continuance? If so, what’s the best strategy and what are the deadlines?

Case theory / elements and strategy
- What legal claims are being asserted against me and what elements must the plaintiff prove for each claim?
- What is our legal theory/defense? What facts support it?
- What is the likely range of outcomes or remedies (damages, injunctions, attorneys’ fees)?
- How strong is the opposing party’s case from our perspective? What are their major weaknesses?

Evidence & discovery
- What specific documents and witnesses do we need to produce or subpoena?
- What evidence will be most persuasive to the judge?
- Are there specific emails/texts/call logs I should highlight or pull out?
- Will we need expert witnesses (e.g., damages expert, contract interpretation) and when must they be disclosed?

Witness prep & testimony
- Which witnesses will we call and how should they testify?
- Can we do mock questioning or prepare witness statements?
- How should I answer difficult factual questions? (Practice short, factual, non-speculative answers.)

Communication & settlement
- Should we engage in settlement talks or mediation now? What’s the best timing?
- What settlement range would you recommend and what are our bottom-line terms?
- If settlement is discussed in court, how would that be handled?

Costs & logistics
- What are estimated costs if this goes to trial (attorney hours, expert fees, filing costs)?
- How long is the hearing likely to last and who should attend?
- Do we need to file exhibit lists or pre-mark exhibits before the hearing?

Privilege, confidentiality & communications
- Which communications are covered by attorney-client privilege?
- Are there communications I should avoid (social media, discussion with friends or third parties)?
- Should we seek a protective order for sensitive documents?

Appeals & next steps
- If we lose at the preliminary hearing, what are our options and timelines for appeal?
- What are critical dates I should calendar now?

Likely questions the judge may ask (examples and why)
- “Are both parties ready to proceed?” (Court wants case status and readiness.)
- “Have the parties attempted to resolve the dispute?” (Judge checks for settlement efforts.)
- “Do you agree on the relevant facts / are there agreed-upon issues?” (To narrow issues.)
- “Is the complaint timely? Was service proper?” (Jurisdiction and timeliness issues.)
- “What relief are you requesting?” (Clarifies remedies sought.)
- “What evidence will you present to prove X element?” (Seeks to understand proof plan.)
How to prepare: have succinct answers, cite key dates and documents, and ask your lawyer first if you should speak or let counsel respond.

Likely questions opposing counsel might ask (in court or during discovery)
- Questions about contract terms: “When did you sign this agreement? What did paragraph X require?”
- Performance and breach: “Did you complete the obligations? What was the employer’s response?”
- Damages and mitigation: “How did you calculate these damages? What steps did you take to mitigate?”
- Communications: “Did you send or receive message X on date Y?” (Have originals.)
- Credibility challenges: “Have you ever been terminated or disciplined before?” (Be truthful and concise.)
How to prepare: review documents and your timeline; coordinate with Maria on consistent testimony and what admissions to avoid.

Practical tips for testimony and hearings
- Answer only the question asked. Keep answers short and factual. Don’t volunteer extra details.
- If you don’t know or don’t recall, say so. Do not guess.
- If you need to confer with counsel, ask to do so rather than answering under pressure.
- Dress conservatively, arrive early, and bring multiple copies of exhibits and a notebook with your timeline.
- Request exhibits be pre-marked and exchanged per local rules; ask Maria to handle exhibit handling.

Documents and exhibits to prepare (checklist)
- The contract(s) at issue, any amendments, offer letters, or written agreements.
- All written communications (emails, texts, letters) with the employer/ex-spouse relevant to the dispute.
- Payment records, invoices, bank statements, receipts.
- Personnel records, performance reviews, warnings, termination notices (if relevant).
- Timeline of events with dates and short notes.
- Witness contact list and summary statements.
- Copies of the complaint, summons, and any motions/filings served on you.

Preservation & digital evidence
- Save and back up emails, text messages, call logs, and social media posts. Consider producing screenshots plus native files if requested.
- Put a hold on deletion of relevant devices/accounts and tell anyone who might have relevant records to preserve them.

Requests to make of your attorney now
- Ask Maria to run a mock hearing or Q&A with you.
- Request a written list of what to bring and any pleadings you must sign.
- Ask for a clear plan for discovery: what we will request and what we expect to be asked to produce.
- Confirm who will speak in court and whether you will testify at the preliminary hearing.

Safety and communications
- Do not message the opposing party or post about the case on social media.
- If opposing counsel contacts you, refer them to Maria and do not respond without counsel present or direction.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and requesting a prep meeting? OR
- Create a printable checklist (PDF-style text) you can bring to the meeting? OR
- Help you organize your documents into a timeline and exhibit list (I can provide a template you can fill in)?

